WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.070
In the comics, the Avengers take on a little
bit of everything -- from alien hordes to

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.689
hungry vampires, they've faced it all.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.470
But the buffet of beasts and villains found
on the page meant that the movies would have

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.860
to reconcile all that fantasy with at least
some semblance of reality.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:21.420
It's one thing to have gods, robots and wizards
rubbing elbows in a comic book, but in live-action

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.850
films striving to be taken seriously, that's
a bit more difficult.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:30.160
The solution was a consistent theme throughout
the MCU films: the notion that in the Marvel

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.689
world, magic and science are the same.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.931
The characters we might otherwise perceive
as gods or magical monsters are just aliens.

00:00:36.931 --> 00:00:42.239
A flying hammer and a rainbow bridge are no
different than one of Rocket Raccoon's cool

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:43.239
gadgets.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:46.999
The idea is to ground everything in some basis
of reality.

00:00:46.999 --> 00:00:51.659
With the release of Thor: Ragnarok, though,
all that changed — and with it, some fundamental

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:54.999
aspects of the Marvel Cinematic Universe were
scrubbed out.

00:00:54.999 --> 00:00:59.319
The movie was so much fun, no one seemed to
notice, but here's a spoiler filled look at

00:00:59.319 --> 00:01:03.010
how Thor: Ragnarok may have radically changed
the MCU.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.540
"It's not possible."

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.500
"Darling you have no idea what's possible."

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.040
Not gods

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.780
In the comics, there's no argument: Thor is
a god.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.610
So are Odin and a bunch of other characters.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.460
And it never seems to bother anyone that all
these gods of Greek and Norse mythology are

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.050
hanging out in New York City having brawls.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.170
This hasn't been the case in the films.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:34.780
In 2011's Thor it was implied that Asgardians
were not gods, but aliens mistaken as gods

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.780
by the Vikings.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.640
You could argue there was some debate on the
issue outside of Thor.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.400
While Thor never calls himself a god in early
movies, plenty of other characters do.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.560
"Oh, I still don't think you're the God of
Thunder.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.390
But you ought to be."

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.550
Black Widow, Tony Stark, and Nick Fury all
call him a god.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:03.100
But they're all members of the same "primitive"
culture unable to comprehend the Asgardians,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.940
so you have to take their assessment with
a grain of salt.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.379
Meanwhile, even though Loki refers to himself
as a god, considering his arrogance, he'd

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.900
likely call himself that whether he was from
Asgard or Hoboken.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.420
"Puny god."

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.999
By the first few minutes of 2013's The Dark
World, the matter seems settled as Odin says:

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:29.340
"We are not gods.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.459
We are born.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.000
We live.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.020
We die.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.480
Just as humans do."

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.989
So, even if the earlier films left the matter
of their heritage unanswered, The Dark World

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:43.639
told us once and for all … or at least for
a little while.

00:02:43.639 --> 00:02:44.639
Science and magic

00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:49.810
At the same time, the MCU's filmmakers seemed
to want to prepare audiences for the synergy

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:55.340
between science and magic they would see in
Avengers by laying the groundwork in Thor

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.060
and Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.920
In Thor, the Odinson tells Jane Foster:

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.950
"Your ancestors called it magic and you call
it science.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.980
Well, I come from a place where they're one
and the same thing."

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.659
He goes on to show Foster in her own notes
how the World Tree of Norse mythology connects

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:16.040
with her own theories of the universe.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.150
In the post-credits scene, when Erik meets
Nick Fury, the latter unveils the Tesseract,

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.150
saying:

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.790
"Legend tells us one thing, history another.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.010
But every now and then we find something that
belongs to both."

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:35.010
According to the Red Skull, this understanding
of the synergy between magic and science is

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.889
precisely what makes him superior to others.

00:03:37.889 --> 00:03:42.439
As he toys with the Tower Keeper who guards
the Tesseract, he tells them they are alike

00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:43.439
because:

00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:49.269
"What others see as superstition, you and
I know to be a science."

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:53.510
Later, when Hitler sends his underlings to
get an update from the Red Skull, one of the

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:58.709
Nazis scoffs at the villain, asking him if
he plans to win the war with "magic."

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:01.349
The Red Skull corrects him, saying:

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:02.349
"Science.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:05.699
But I understand your confusion.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:08.439
Great power has always baffled primitive men."

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:13.519
The merging of science and magic is a consistent
theme, even when it's a subtle suggestion

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.099
rather than a blatant statement.

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:20.590
In Thor: The Dark World, when Thor brings
Jane Foster to Asgard to find out what's happened

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:25.940
to her, Foster is subjected to tests directed
by an Asgardian woman named Eir.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.020
Foster thinks she recognizes what's happening
and asks:

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.260
"That's a quantum field generator, isn't it?"

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.690
Eir then tries to her it's a Soul Forge, like
that's something unique to Asgard, but Jane

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:37.690
then asks:

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.900
"Does this Soul Forge transfer molecular energy
from one place to another?"

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:44.580
"Yes."

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.020
"Quantum field generator."

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.730
The Soul Forge and other Asgardian tech is
operated with the same motion and touch methods

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:57.540
we not only see Tony Stark using in Iron Man,
but that we see in the everyday lives of aliens

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:02.980
in Guardians of the Galaxy and the vibranium-enhanced
Wakanda of Black Panther.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:08.110
Even in Doctor Strange, arguably the magic
heart of the MCU, the connections between

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.230
science and magic are clear.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:15.080
When Strange first meets her, the Ancient
One shows him a chakra chart, an acupuncture

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.270
chart, and an MRI scan.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.610
She tells him that each was created by someone
who could see "in part, but not the whole."

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:27.230
She invites him to think of magic spells as
"programs," describing them as "The source

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.080
code that shapes reality."

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.970
When Strange asks how he could possibly ever
learn to do what she does, the Ancient One

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.240
responds by asking him how he was able to
be a neurosurgeon.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:42.720
In other words, magic and science go hand-in-hand
in the MCU, making it simpler for audiences

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.890
to understand and accept it.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.480
But then...

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.140
Things change

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.380
With Thor: Ragnarok, everything changed as
the concept of science equating with magic

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.100
got kicked to the wayside for a much more
mythical approach.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.980
From the beginning, the change is clear.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.350
Surtur's rantings about the coming of Ragnarok
are all about prophecy, and prophecy hasn't

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.980
reared its head in the MCU before now.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.300
Without any kind of explanation, suddenly
the Asgardians are gods.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:16.610
Thor, Loki, Odin, and Hela all refer to themselves
and others as gods.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.880
"I'm the goddess of death…

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.900
What were you the god of again?"

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:27.160
In a psychic conversation with Odin, complaining
he can't defeat Hela without his hammer, Odin

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.460
asks Thor:

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.800
"Are you Thor, the god of hammers?"

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.830
Doctor Strange calls Thor the "god of thunder"
when he meets him and Thor refers to himself

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.290
the same way several times.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:41.020
He even tells Loki:

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.370
"You'll always be the god of mischief."

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:47.370
The about-face is stark enough to give any
audience member whiplash.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.280
And it's not just about the status of Odin
&amp; company either.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.040
This untethering from the rule that science
and reason have to inform everything that

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.590
happens in the MCU has another obvious effect
in the film.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:02.190
"I'm into numbers and science and stuff!"
(scoffs)

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:07.280
Doctor Strange was released only a year before
Thor: Ragnarok, yet in the scene when Thor

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.770
finds himself in the Greenwich Village Sanctum
Sanctorum, magic already works much differently

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.160
than it did in Strange's own film.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:15.860
"Take a seat."

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.520
In Doctor Strange, magic is portrayed as following
specific rules, which lends itself to that

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:27.110
notion of science being intrinsically connected
to it.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:31.410
Almost everything the different magic users
do begins with the fiery patterns they make

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:37.360
in the air, or in mystical items imbued with
power like the sling rings or Strange's Cloak

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.590
of Levitation.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:43.300
But when Thor and Strange meet in Thor: Ragnarok,
things work differently.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.800
Strange teleports himself and Thor throughout
the house at a pace that disorients and annoys

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.900
Thor, without using his sling ring or making
even the slightest motion with his hand.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:57.810
Thor finds himself inexplicably holding a
cup of tea and then a bottomless mug of beer.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:02.840
In Strange's own film these things would seem
impossible, yet here magic is being handled

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.170
in the more undefinable way we see in older
comics.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:10.280
By dropping the pretense that science must
be the base of all supernatural elements of

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.720
the film, the characters are free to lean
into their talents, no matter how unusual

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.139
they are.

00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:17.139
Open ends

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:23.050
Ironically, even though Thor: Ragnarok ends
the established godlessness of Asgard and

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:28.139
the magic-science synergy, much of what makes
the film so fun is exactly what it's throwing

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:29.139
away.

00:08:29.139 --> 00:08:33.710
Thor's adventures on the planet Sakaar give
us some of the best moments of the film.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:39.020
It's where Thor battles the Hulk, where we
meet the hilarious Grandmaster and the tough-as-nails

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:40.250
Valkyrie.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:45.000
And of course it's where we learn about Thor
and Loki's well-loved childhood game, "Get

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.000
Help."

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.030
None of it would make sense without everything
that's come before — and everything Ragnarok

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.590
is willfully forgetting.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:56.490
The fact that the Asgardians were established
as aliens instead of gods is precisely why

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:02.380
Thor, Loki, and Valkyrie are not as out of
their element as they otherwise might be.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:06.790
Thor isn't shocked by meeting a group of aliens
moments after he arrives on Sakaar.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:08.940
He isn't confused by their technology.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.680
Valkyrie clearly has an intuitive command
over her spaceship.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:17.700
Loki navigates the technology so well he's
able to steal security codes from the Grandmaster.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.740
Odin's sons wield laser rifles as comfortably
as we've seen them wield hammers and daggers

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.510
respectively, and when Thor steals the Grandmaster's
ship, he says it should be no problem to figure

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.990
out because after all, "it's just another
spaceship."

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:32.820
Indeed, he handles it just fine.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.710
Even Bruce Banner, with all his doctorates,
is utterly shocked by the fact that he's on

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:42.290
an alien planet, stressing him out to the
point where he almost turns back into the

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.910
Other Guy over it.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:46.860
Yet the comparably meatheaded Thor is just
fine.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.450
As if he were not a god, but just another
alien, exactly like we've been told up until

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.450
now.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:56.270
We're also left to wonder who else might be
a supposed god in the story.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:01.190
In the comics, it isn't just Thor and Loki
and the named characters of Asgard who are

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.190
gods.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:03.780
Everyone on Asgard is a god.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:10.029
Skurge the Executioner, Lady Sif, the Warriors
Three, Heimdall, and all the rest.

00:10:10.029 --> 00:10:11.460
Every man, woman, and child.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:16.620
If there's an Asgardian dude who cleans the
toilets of Asgard, then the dude who cleans

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.020
the toilets of Asgard is a god.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.420
Does this mean all the Asgardians of the MCU
are gods?

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:27.279
If it does, then why are they such cannon
fodder when it comes to fighting Hela's zombies

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:28.830
or Dark Elves?

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:33.420
How could there be such a thing in Asgard
as an innocent civilian or bystander?

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.940
If they're gods, they should all be kicking
butt and taking names.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.420
Meanwhile, it might not make sense that Loki
is called a god.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:46.460
If the character is actually a Frost Giant
adopted by Asgardians, how is he a god?

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.170
Are you a god if you just hang out with other
gods?

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.130
The questions are endless.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:55.720
Then there's the matter of how the changes
Thor: Ragnarok made to the MCU will impact

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:56.980
future movies.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:01.779
In spite of its huge cast, Avengers: Infinity
War should give Thor a key role in the fight

00:11:01.779 --> 00:11:03.130
against Thanos.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:07.760
Thanos, even without the Infinity Stones,
is supposedly the most powerful being in the

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:08.760
universe.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:13.800
If Thor is now a god, then he's probably going
to be the only one even able to keep up with

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:14.920
the Mad Titan.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:15.920
"Ready?"

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:20.740
If not, we may have to wonder whether the
developments in Ragnarok might harm the integrity

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.130
of the MCU.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:27.330
If Marvel Studios decides to ultimately nix
the whole concept of Asgardian gods in a future

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.880
feature, it could make things even more confusing
and convoluted.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:34.880
On the other hand, allowing creators the freedom
to change the rules once in a while is what

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.830
makes the comics such a rush to read, so who
knows?

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:41.589
Maybe this abrupt alteration is just part
of a bigger plan to shake things up in the

00:11:41.589 --> 00:11:47.120
Marvel Cinematic Universe and bring in some
real magic, science and continuity be darned.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:48.310
Thanks for watching!

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.820
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:52.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

